<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaston IV of Béarn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1131</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Gaston IV the Crusader</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaston IV, Viscount of Béarn, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a>, took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> and was among the leaders at the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1099. He later fought alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>, helping take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in 1118, and was killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> in 1131.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaston-iv-of-bearn.jpg" alt="Gaston IV of Béarn" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexandre Lacauchie · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaston_dans_J%C3%A9rusalem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gaston, born around 1060, was the son of Centulle V of Béarn and Beatrix of Bigorre, and he succeeded his father as viscount in 1090. During his rule the borders of Béarn were fixed more firmly: he defeated the viscount of Dax, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orthez">Orthez</a>, Pays de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixe">Mixe</a> and Ostabaret by 1105, and gained Montaner by his marriage to Talesa, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ramirez">Sancho Ramírez</a>, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a>. Though technically a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Duchy of Aquitaine, then ruled by William IX, he made Béarn in effect an autonomous territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1096 Gaston led a Béarnais contingent in the First Crusade under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond IV of Toulouse</a>, carrying his own standard and commanding his own men. At the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> of 1097–1098 he led one of the divisions in the final battle against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerbogha">Kerbogha</a>, the atabeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, and afterwards left Raymond for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a>. He and Tancred were sent ahead of the main army to occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, and at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> he was in charge of Godfrey's siege engines. He entered the city on 15 July 1099. He and Tancred sheltered some of its Muslims in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aqsa-mosque">Al-Aqsa Mosque</a>, but other crusaders killed them, to their fury.</p><p>In August 1099 he led part of the centre of the crusader army at the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> and then went home. In Béarn he oversaw the building of many churches to shelter pilgrims on the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> and gave property to the abbey of St. Foy for new buildings in Morlàas, though he also upheld his claims against the abbey of St. Vincent de Lucq and the monastery of St. Mont. With Centule II of Bigorre he took part in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a> in 1118, and the king made him lord of the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gaston was killed in battle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> against the Almoravid governor of that city. His young son Centulle VI succeeded him, with Talesa as regent. She wished to unite Béarn and Aragon, then roughly equal in power, but Aragon united with Catalonia instead and Béarn began to decline. His descendants Gaston VI and Gaston VII took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seventh-crusade">Seventh Crusade</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gaston IV of Béarn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aided by <b>Gaston IV of Béarn</b> and other southern French lords, he took Zaragoza after a siege of about seven months in 1118, defeated an Almoravid army at the Battle of Cutanda in 1120 and in 1125–1126 led a raid deep into Andalusia, returning with thousands of Mozarab…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gaston IV</b> took part in the First Crusade and helped Aragon conquer Zaragoza in 1118.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-bearn">Viscounty of Béarn</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.47,-0.38&amp;year=1121" aria-label="Valencia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Gaston IV of Béarn"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4975 4194h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4975 4194h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.47,-0.38&amp;year=1121">What was here in 1121? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
